WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.359
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll

00:00:03.359 --> 00:00:06.150
describe independent work with Runnable, start

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.941
separate threads, and join them before reading

00:00:08.941 --> 00:00:09.790
their results.

00:00:10.433 --> 00:00:13.534
Clear ownership and completion rules let larger

00:00:13.534 --> 00:00:16.371
applications prepare independent parts of a

00:00:16.371 --> 00:00:19.405
report while ensuring the caller uses finished

00:00:19.405 --> 00:00:19.933
results.

00:00:20.582 --> 00:00:23.468
A campus welcome desk needs two preparation

00:00:23.468 --> 00:00:23.938
counts.

00:00:23.938 --> 00:00:26.756
One job models two badge checks; the other

00:00:26.756 --> 00:00:28.501
models four supply checks.

00:00:28.501 --> 00:00:31.655
Each loop iteration adds one completed check to

00:00:31.655 --> 00:00:33.065
that job's own count.

00:00:33.065 --> 00:00:36.152
These are small model counts, not actual badge

00:00:36.152 --> 00:00:38.232
inspections or file operations.

00:00:38.885 --> 00:00:41.154
We'll start both workers, wait for both to

00:00:41.154 --> 00:00:43.532
finish, and then report the first count, the

00:00:43.532 --> 00:00:46.126
second count, and whether either worker is still

00:00:46.126 --> 00:00:46.450
alive.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:48.719
The caller will print in that fixed order.

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:51.259
After the worked run, you'll predict the report

00:00:51.259 --> 00:00:53.799
for different job limits; the output will still

00:00:53.799 --> 00:00:55.960
not tell us which worker finished first.

00:00:56.636 --> 00:00:59.495
Let's open Main.java in the Workspace and give

00:00:59.495 --> 00:01:01.360
this program a starting point.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:04.219
Then we'll define the work before creating the

00:01:04.219 --> 00:01:05.711
workers that execute it.

00:01:09.723 --> 00:01:12.511
Main is the class containing the program, and

00:01:12.511 --> 00:01:15.361
its main method starts the caller's execution.

00:01:15.361 --> 00:01:17.777
Waiting with join can throw the checked

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:19.945
InterruptedException, so this small

00:01:19.945 --> 00:01:22.423
demonstration declares that possibility.

00:01:22.423 --> 00:01:25.087
Our expected report follows the normal path

00:01:25.087 --> 00:01:26.698
where both waits complete.

00:01:37.332 --> 00:01:40.299
Each preparation job needs its own limit and

00:01:40.299 --> 00:01:40.771
result.

00:01:40.771 --> 00:01:43.603
We'll define CountJob here inside the main

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:46.436
method, using Runnable to specify its work

00:01:46.436 --> 00:01:47.110
operation.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:50.347
The constructor records the requested checks and

00:01:50.347 --> 00:01:52.707
starts that object's total at zero.

00:02:11.179 --> 00:02:14.076
The two fields belong to each CountJob object.

00:02:14.076 --> 00:02:17.037
Private limits direct field access, but it does

00:02:17.037 --> 00:02:19.682
not by itself keep threads from sharing an

00:02:19.682 --> 00:02:20.123
object.

00:02:20.123 --> 00:02:23.147
Our design will give each worker a separate job.

00:02:23.147 --> 00:02:26.044
Let's supply the run method that performs that

00:02:26.044 --> 00:02:27.304
job's counting work.

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:51.886
The loop begins at zero and adds one for each

00:02:51.886 --> 00:02:53.370
index below the limit.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:56.002
Override checks that this public method

00:02:56.002 --> 00:02:58.363
implements the interface operation.

00:02:58.363 --> 00:03:01.466
Defining run has not started a worker; it only

00:03:01.466 --> 00:03:04.300
describes what a later invocation will do.

00:03:04.331 --> 00:03:06.919
The caller will need to read the completed

00:03:06.919 --> 00:03:09.814
total, so let's add a getter and finish the job

00:03:09.814 --> 00:03:10.492
definition.

00:03:10.492 --> 00:03:13.449
The getter returns a value; it will not wait for

00:03:13.449 --> 00:03:14.681
the work on its own.

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:25.275
Now let's create separate jobs for the two badge

00:03:25.275 --> 00:03:27.216
checks and four supply checks.

00:03:27.216 --> 00:03:30.063
Each receives its own limit and zero result,

00:03:30.063 --> 00:03:32.845
which keeps their changing totals separate.

00:03:40.569 --> 00:03:43.419
First and second identify the work objects.

00:03:43.419 --> 00:03:46.534
To request separate execution, we also need two

00:03:46.534 --> 00:03:49.185
Thread objects, each associated with its

00:03:49.185 --> 00:03:50.377
corresponding job.

00:03:50.377 --> 00:03:53.360
Constructing those workers still does not run

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:54.619
the counting loops.

00:04:03.259 --> 00:04:06.149
We have two jobs and two prepared execution

00:04:06.149 --> 00:04:06.552
paths.

00:04:06.552 --> 00:04:09.644
Let's start both before waiting for either, so

00:04:09.644 --> 00:04:12.870
both requests exist before the caller begins its

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:13.609
first wait.

00:04:19.656 --> 00:04:22.352
Start requests that a worker execute its run

00:04:22.352 --> 00:04:24.619
method while the caller can continue.

00:04:24.619 --> 00:04:27.131
Calling a job's run method directly would

00:04:27.131 --> 00:04:29.765
instead be an ordinary call on the caller's

00:04:29.765 --> 00:04:30.194
thread.

00:04:30.194 --> 00:04:33.135
Starting allows concurrent progress; it does not

00:04:33.135 --> 00:04:35.585
guarantee simultaneous execution, faster

00:04:35.585 --> 00:04:38.281
completion, or a particular finishing order.

00:04:38.312 --> 00:04:41.289
The report needs finished values, so let's join

00:04:41.289 --> 00:04:42.555
each started worker.

00:04:42.555 --> 00:04:45.595
Joining the first concerns only that worker; the

00:04:45.595 --> 00:04:48.382
second may keep progressing while the caller

00:04:48.382 --> 00:04:48.762
waits.

00:04:54.118 --> 00:04:56.970
When an untimed join on a started worker returns

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:59.585
normally, that worker has terminated and its

00:04:59.585 --> 00:05:02.022
earlier writes are visible to the caller.

00:05:02.022 --> 00:05:04.517
Both joins are needed before treating both

00:05:04.517 --> 00:05:05.468
totals as final.

00:05:05.468 --> 00:05:07.964
If a join exits through interruption, that

00:05:07.964 --> 00:05:10.816
exception does not establish completion and this

00:05:10.816 --> 00:05:12.718
expected report does not follow.

00:05:12.746 --> 00:05:15.791
Let's print each job's finished total, then ask

00:05:15.791 --> 00:05:17.799
whether either worker is alive.

00:05:17.799 --> 00:05:20.843
This observation is meaningful after the starts

00:05:20.843 --> 00:05:23.953
and joins: false by itself could also describe a

00:05:23.953 --> 00:05:25.896
thread that was never started.

00:05:49.202 --> 00:05:52.078
The caller's print statements fix the report

00:05:52.078 --> 00:05:52.470
order.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:55.411
Each worker changed only its own job's total,

00:05:55.411 --> 00:05:58.026
and both waits now establish completion.

00:05:58.026 --> 00:06:00.902
Let's click Run to see the completed counts.

00:06:06.264 --> 00:06:08.909
First is two because that job performed two

00:06:08.909 --> 00:06:09.585
increments.

00:06:09.585 --> 00:06:11.984
Second is four because its separate job

00:06:11.984 --> 00:06:12.906
performed four.

00:06:12.906 --> 00:06:15.736
Workers alive is false after both workers have

00:06:15.736 --> 00:06:16.412
terminated.

00:06:16.412 --> 00:06:19.303
These lines come from the caller; listing First

00:06:19.303 --> 00:06:22.070
before Second does not tell us which counting

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:23.239
job finished first.

00:06:23.291 --> 00:06:26.079
Let's change the two job limits to three badge

00:06:26.079 --> 00:06:27.837
checks and two supply checks.

00:06:27.837 --> 00:06:30.201
The ownership, starts, joins, and print

00:06:30.201 --> 00:06:31.716
statements stay the same.

00:06:31.716 --> 00:06:34.080
Running the complete program again will

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.989
construct fresh jobs and fresh Thread objects; a

00:06:36.989 --> 00:06:39.838
Thread object itself cannot be started a second

00:06:39.838 --> 00:06:40.141
time.

00:06:48.383 --> 00:06:51.203
Before running, predict all three report lines

00:06:51.203 --> 00:06:52.368
for the new limits.

00:06:52.368 --> 00:06:55.065
Then decide whether the report can establish

00:06:55.065 --> 00:06:56.782
which worker finished first.

00:06:56.782 --> 00:06:59.602
Use the loop counts, each normal join, and the

00:06:59.602 --> 00:07:02.422
caller's print order as separate parts of your

00:07:02.422 --> 00:07:03.158
explanation.

00:07:11.239 --> 00:07:14.188
Let's click Run and compare your prediction with

00:07:14.188 --> 00:07:17.014
the actual report from these new work objects.

00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:25.069
First is now three and Second is two, matching

00:07:25.069 --> 00:07:26.223
the two new limits.

00:07:26.223 --> 00:07:28.713
Neither worker is alive after both joins.

00:07:28.713 --> 00:07:31.446
The values changed because the requested work

00:07:31.446 --> 00:07:34.362
changed, while the same ownership and completion

00:07:34.362 --> 00:07:37.034
rules kept the results ready for the caller.

00:07:37.034 --> 00:07:39.585
The report still does not establish worker

00:07:39.585 --> 00:07:40.375
finish order.

00:07:40.437 --> 00:07:42.676
You separated work descriptions from execution

00:07:42.676 --> 00:07:44.915
paths, started independent workers, and waited

00:07:44.915 --> 00:07:46.326
before reading their results.

00:07:46.326 --> 00:07:48.419
Each worker owned its changing total, and a

00:07:48.419 --> 00:07:50.269
normal join established completion and

00:07:50.269 --> 00:07:50.804
visibility.

00:07:50.804 --> 00:07:52.994
For a transfer task, describe two independent

00:07:52.994 --> 00:07:54.601
jobs in a report you might build.

00:07:54.601 --> 00:07:56.937
Identify each job's state, where the caller must

00:07:56.937 --> 00:07:59.176
wait, and why the final report order would not

00:07:59.176 --> 00:08:01.512
prove simultaneous execution or finishing order.
